course: dictionary
lesson: 13395
title: Heighten: making things stronger
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you heighten something, you make it stronger or greater. Most often it is a feeling, like fear or excitement, that grows. «The creak of the door heightened her fear.» The creak did not make the door taller. It made her fear grow bigger.
explain: Heighten is a verb, so its ending changes with time: heightens (now), heightened (already happened), heightening (happening now). «The dark sky heightens the tension before the storm.» Ask yourself: is a feeling, risk or danger getting bigger? If yes, heighten is probably the word.
explain: Heighten can also mean to make something higher or taller, but that is rare in everyday talk. Good clues are words like fear, tension, danger, risk or awareness. Those words point to stronger feelings or greater risks, not height.
fact: «Heighten» means to make a feeling, effect or danger stronger or greater.
fact: «Heightened» means made stronger, as in «heightened fear» or «heightened risk».
fact: Common phrases are heighten the tension, heighten awareness and heightened senses.
fact: «Smoke heightens the danger of fire.» Smoke makes the danger of fire greater.
fact: Heighten is more common in stories, news and safety talk than in everyday chat.
exercise: «The silence heightened her fear.» What does heightened mean here? | Made quieter | Made to fall | *Made stronger | Made shorter
exercise: «Heavy rain heightens the risk of floods.» What happens to the risk? | It moves away | It gets smaller | It stops | *It gets bigger
exercise: «The guard's heightened awareness helped him spot the thief.» What does heightened awareness mean? | *Being more alert and noticing more | Being sleepy | Being asleep | Forgetting things
test: «The loud drumming heightened the excitement of the crowd.» What did the drumming do? | Made the crowd quiet | *Made the excitement stronger | Made the drum smaller | Made the crowd leave
test: Sam says heighten always means making something taller. Is he right? | *No, it usually means making a feeling or danger stronger | No, it means to whisper | Yes, always | Yes, but only for trees
exam: «Smoke heightens the danger of fire, so the firefighters acted fast.» What does heightens mean in this sentence? | Hides | Puts out | *Makes stronger | Cools down
